            <ArticleTitle>Pogosta disease: clinical observations during an outbreak in the province of North Karelia, Finland.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1177-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To characterize the clinical picture of Pogosta disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">The data of 73 patients who had had Pogosta disease in 1981 and who then had been seen by a local physician in North Karelia were analysed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The main manifestations were fever (23%), rash (88%) and joint symptoms (93%). The joint symptoms in some patients lasted for several months and were severe enough to cause immobilization. The clinical picture was identical in those patients who had a definite serological diagnosis and those who did not have a detectable antibody response.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The symptoms of Sindbis virus-induced Pogosta discase consist of fever, rash and joint symptoms, whic may be severe and prolonged.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
